#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --script
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.14"
# dependencies = [
#   "click>=8.1.0",
# ]
# ///

"""Regenerate port/manifest.tsv and port/cut.tsv from the vendored libslic3r tree.

Walk every ``.h``, ``.hpp``, ``.cpp`` and ``.mm`` file under
``vendor/OrcaSlicer/src/libslic3r`` and write one manifest row per file: path, subsystem,
lines, the file-level include-DAG level (task 03-manifest-file-set); in scope or out with a
reason, and pipeline stage or none (task 06-manifest-scope); crate-level level, target crate
and target module (task 07-crate-cut); the six judgement columns -- template-heavy, equality
tier, tolerance, comparison function, proof path, fuzz target (task 14-manifest-columns, this
task; see "Judgement columns" below); and status, derived fresh from the Rust tree on every run
(see ``compute_status``).

The row set always comes from the filesystem walk, never from
``src/libslic3r/CMakeLists.txt``. That build file is parsed only to cross-check that every
file it names also has a row, because the build file itself omits files that are produced by
other means (an orphan pass, dead code still compiled in, Apple-only sources).

``port/cut.tsv`` is the named feedback-arc cut of the module graph (not the file graph, which
is already acyclic): see the "Crate cut" section below for the module derivation, the cut
algorithm, and the file's own schema (``CUT_HEADER``).

**A port task never edits port/manifest.tsv.** Every column but the six judgement columns is
recomputed from the vendored tree on every run and overwrites whatever is committed; the six
judgement columns are seeded once (the first time a row's six cells are all still empty) and
then carried forward verbatim forever after, by hand in review or by 35-trial-run, never by
this script; and ``status`` is derived fresh from the Rust tree on every run and is never read
as an input -- a row's status advances because a task added a Rust file, not because anybody
wrote a cell, which is what lets a hundred task branches run at once without colliding on one
line of one file. ``just inventory-check`` (``--check`` below) enforces exactly this: it diffs
the derived columns only, ignores the six judgement columns and ``status`` entirely, and fails
only when a derived column has drifted from the vendored tree.
"""

